# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
imagine_unperceived	one
frame_ideas_omit	one
perceive_while	one
conceive_mind_indep	one
conceive_unconceived	two	unconceived	A1 C1	conceiving objects as existing with no one perceiving them (what is pictured)	objects actually being unthought of while one is thinking of them (the act of conceiving)
repugnant	one
# Unsure whether to count conceive_unconceived as two senses. The text's "existing unconceived or unthought of" can describe what is pictured (no perceiver in the scene) or the act (the object is not thought of at all).
# In P3 the claim follows from conceiving a mind-independent existence. That makes it the "what is pictured" sense, which is consistent and not repugnant.
# In P4, A1 and C1 the denial rests on the manifest repugnancy. That makes it the act sense: you cannot conceive something while it goes unconceived by you.
# No single P or A joins two claims in the two senses, so I name no bridge. The shift lies inside the shared claim between P3 and C1.
